update articles set
  title_en = $t$Spain Travel Guide: Andalusia, Madrid, Barcelona and Ferry Tips$t$,
  excerpt_en = $e$Plan your trip to Spain: the Alhambra, Córdoba, Seville, Madrid and Barcelona, the best time to go, budgets, how to get there and visa basics.$e$,
  body_en = $b$<p>Spain is one of the most visited countries in the world, and it is easy to see why. Moorish palaces, Gothic cathedrals, Gaudí's architecture, long Mediterranean beaches and a tradition of late, sociable meals all fit into one country, linked by one of Europe's best high-speed rail networks. This guide covers the regions worth visiting, the best time to go, budgets, how to get there from Europe, the Middle East, the Americas and Asia-Pacific, and the entry rules you should check before you travel.</p>

<h2>Why Visit Spain</h2>
<p>Spain offers great variety within one country. You can explore the Islamic heritage of Al-Andalus, the art museums of Madrid, Gaudí's Barcelona, the Mediterranean beaches of Valencia and the Costa del Sol, and the green north of Galicia and the Basque Country. Its high-speed rail network makes it easy to combine several cities in one trip, and the food, from tapas to paella, is a reason to visit on its own.</p>

<h2>Andalusia: The Heritage of Al-Andalus</h2>
<h3>Granada and the Alhambra</h3>
<p>The Alhambra, the palace-fortress of the Nasrid dynasty, is the highlight of many trips to Spain. Its Nasrid Palaces, the Court of the Lions and the Generalife gardens are UNESCO-listed alongside the Albaicín, Granada's old Moorish quarter, which climbs the hill opposite. Tickets are limited and timed and often sell out weeks ahead in high season, so book only through the official channel as early as you can.</p>
<h3>Córdoba</h3>
<p>The Mosque-Cathedral of Córdoba, with its forest of red-and-white arches, is one of the most important monuments of Islamic architecture in Europe. Spend time in the historic centre, the old Jewish quarter and, in May, the flower-filled patios during the city's patio festival.</p>
<h3>Seville</h3>
<p>Seville combines the Real Alcázar, a royal palace built largely in the Mudéjar style, the cathedral and its Giralda tower, which was originally a minaret, and the riverside Triana district, known for flamenco and ceramics. Spring brings Holy Week processions and the April Fair, and both are crowded, so book well ahead.</p>
<h3>Málaga and the Costa del Sol</h3>
<p>Málaga is a lively city with one of Spain's busiest airports. It has a Moorish fortress (the Alcazaba), good museums and easy access to beach towns along the coast. It also makes a convenient base for day trips to Ronda or Granada.</p>

<h2>Beyond Andalusia</h2>
<h3>Madrid</h3>
<p>Spain's capital is known for its art museums, especially the Prado, the Reina Sofía and the Thyssen-Bornemisza, as well as the Royal Palace, Retiro Park and a food scene that runs late into the night. Toledo and Segovia are both easy day trips by train.</p>
<h3>Barcelona</h3>
<p>Barcelona combines Gaudí's architecture, including the Sagrada Família, Park Güell, Casa Batlló and Casa Milà, with a Gothic old town and a city beach. It is very popular, so book major sights in advance, and watch your belongings in crowded areas and on the metro.</p>
<h3>Valencia and the North</h3>
<p>Valencia is the home of paella and has a futuristic City of Arts and Sciences. In the north, San Sebastián and Bilbao are known for food and modern architecture, while Santiago de Compostela marks the end of the famous pilgrimage route.</p>

<h2>Best Time to Visit Spain</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spring (March to May):</strong> ideal for Andalusia, with flowers, festivals and comfortable temperatures.</li>
<li><strong>Summer (June to August):</strong> beach season. Inland cities such as Seville, Córdoba and Madrid can be extremely hot, so plan sightseeing for early morning and evening.</li>
<li><strong>Autumn (September to November):</strong> warm sea, fewer crowds and pleasant city weather, which makes it one of the best all-round periods.</li>
<li><strong>Winter (December to February):</strong> mild in the south and good value outside the holidays, with snow in the Sierra Nevada and the Pyrenees.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting to Spain</h2>
<p>Madrid and Barcelona are the main international gateways, with Málaga, Alicante, Valencia, Seville, Palma and the Canary Islands also handling many international flights. Routes and airlines change by season, so compare current options.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>From the UK and the rest of Europe:</strong> Spain is one of Europe's best-connected countries, with frequent flights from most major cities, often around two to three hours from London, Paris or Amsterdam. High-speed trains link France with Barcelona, and ferries sail from the UK to northern Spanish ports such as Santander and Bilbao.</li>
<li><strong>From the Gulf and Middle East:</strong> Gulf hubs such as Dubai, Abu Dhabi and Doha have direct flights to Madrid and Barcelona, typically around seven to eight hours.</li>
<li><strong>From North and Latin America:</strong> Madrid is a major hub for flights across the Atlantic. Direct flights from the US East Coast typically take around seven to eight hours.</li>
<li><strong>From Asia-Pacific:</strong> most travellers connect through a Gulf, Asian or European hub. Allow a long day of travel from Australia.</li>
<li><strong>From North Africa:</strong> short ferry crossings link Morocco with southern Spain, for example Tangier to Tarifa or Algeciras, often around one to one and a half hours at sea.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Visas and Entry Requirements</h2>
<p>Spain is part of the Schengen Area, so the same short-stay rules apply as in most of the EU.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>EU/EEA and Swiss citizens</strong> travel with a passport or national ID card.</li>
<li><strong>Visa-exempt visitors</strong>, including citizens of the UK, the USA, Canada, Australia, Japan and many other countries, can usually stay up to 90 days in any 180-day period across the Schengen Area. The EU's Entry/Exit System records non-EU visitors at the border, and the ETIAS travel authorisation is due to follow. Check the official EU website for the current start date.</li>
<li><strong>Other nationalities</strong>, for example Indian, Chinese or Moroccan passport holders, generally need a Schengen short-stay visa unless they hold a valid Schengen residence permit. You will typically need proof of accommodation, return travel, funds and travel medical insurance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Requirements and processing times change, so always check the official website of the Spanish Ministry of Foreign Affairs, your local Spanish consulate and the EU's official travel portals before booking.</p>

<h2>Budget: How Much Does Spain Cost?</h2>
<p>Spain is generally more affordable than France, the UK or Switzerland, but costs rise sharply in summer and in Barcelona, Madrid and coastal resorts. As an <strong>approximate guide per person per day</strong>, excluding flights:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Approximate daily budget</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Budget (hostels, menú del día, public transport)</td><td>about 60–90 EUR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mid-range (3-star hotel, restaurant meals, a few paid sights)</td><td>about 120–200 EUR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Comfort (4–5 star hotels, guided tours, taxis)</td><td>250 EUR and above</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Look for the <strong>menú del día</strong>, a fixed-price lunch menu offered by many restaurants on weekdays, which is often the best-value meal of the day. Some regions and cities also charge a tourist tax on accommodation.</p>

<h2>Halal Food and Muslim-Friendly Travel</h2>
<p>Halal restaurants are easy to find in Granada (especially around the Albaicín), Madrid, Barcelona, Málaga and Valencia, often run by North African or Middle Eastern communities. Spanish menus often include pork (cerdo) and ham (jamón), so ask about ingredients. Seafood, grilled fish, vegetable dishes and many tapas are good alternatives. There are mosques in all the major cities.</p>

<h2>Practical Tips</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meal times:</strong> lunch is often served from around 2 pm and dinner from 8:30 or 9 pm, later than in most other countries.</li>
<li><strong>Trains:</strong> book high-speed trains early for lower fares. Several operators compete on major routes.</li>
<li><strong>Pickpockets:</strong> be vigilant in tourist areas of Barcelona and Madrid, especially on public transport.</li>
<li><strong>Siesta hours:</strong> smaller shops may close in the early afternoon, especially in the south.</li>
<li><strong>Language:</strong> Spanish is spoken everywhere, with Catalan, Basque and Galician in their regions. English is common in tourist areas but less so in small towns.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need a visa to visit Spain?</h3>
<p>It depends on your nationality. Many visitors, including UK, US, Canadian and Australian citizens, can stay up to 90 days without a visa, while others need a Schengen visa. Check official Spanish and EU sources before you book.</p>
<h3>Do I need to book the Alhambra in advance?</h3>
<p>Yes. Daily entries are limited, so book through the official Alhambra ticketing website as early as possible, especially for spring and summer.</p>
<h3>Which is better for a first trip: Andalusia, Madrid or Barcelona?</h3>
<p>For travellers interested in Islamic heritage and historic cities, Andalusia (Granada, Córdoba, Seville) is the natural choice. Barcelona suits architecture and beach lovers, and Madrid suits museum lovers.</p>
<h3>Can I combine Spain with a short trip to Morocco?</h3>
<p>Yes. Ferries from Tarifa and Algeciras make a crossing to Tangier possible as a short add-on. Check the visa rules for both countries and bring your passport.</p>
<h3>Is Spain expensive?</h3>
<p>Spain is mid-priced for Western Europe. It is often cheaper than France, the UK or Scandinavia, though Barcelona, Madrid and summer resorts can be costly.</p>

